--- a/public/chp/blog/io-monad-dao-stream.blog
+++ b/public/chp/blog/io-monad-dao-stream.blog
[036m@@ -6,42 +6,39 @@[0m
                   [:article
                    :$blog-title
                    :$blog-date
[031m-                   [0m
[032m+[0m
                    [:p "In pure functional programming, the IO monad solves a critical problem: how to perform dirty side effects while keeping core logic pure. When a Haskell program returns an IO action, it does not actually execute the network request. It returns a pure, first-class value denoting the effect (well-defined, but not a data structure). The language runtime, acting as the host boundary, takes that value and executes it."]
[031m-                   [0m
[032m+[0m
                    [:p "Conceptually, this is elegant. Practically, standard IO keeps the effect out of the program's reach: an IO value is well-defined, but it is not data. It can be composed or handed to the runtime to be executed; it cannot be taken apart to see the next effect before it runs. For a general action, that description does not even exist until execution computes it. There is no Eq, no Show, no pattern matching: nothing exposes the pending effect as data. The only thing that can run an IO value is the runtime itself (unsafePerformIO is the sanctioned exception)."]
[031m-                   [0m
[032m+[0m
                    [:h2 "IO is Just State Awaiting an Interpreter"]
[031m-                   [0m
[032m+[0m
                    [:p "To understand why this opacity is a limitation, it is necessary to look at the underlying mechanics. Structurally, the IO monad and the State monad are the exact same shape. In Clojure terms, the State monad is just a pure function that takes an immutable value (like a map), and returns a result alongside an updated map. The interpreter lives " [:em "inside"] " the program: evaluating the function allows inspecting the resulting data structure."]
[031m-                   [0m
[032m+[0m
                    [:p "The IO monad operates identically, but the \"state\" it threads is a phantom token representing the physical universe. Because pure functions cannot directly mutate the outside world, the interpreter must live " [:em "outside"] " the language as a privileged host environment. The pure program simply builds a sequence of transitions over this dummy token, and the external host acts as the sole interpreter permitted to execute the actual side-effects. The intent is executed, but the data describing it is swallowed by the runtime."]
[031m-                   [0m
[032m+[0m
                    [:h2 "From Opaque Actions to Transparent Syntax"]
[031m-                   [0m
[032m+[0m
                    [:p "The datom.world architecture rejects opaque abstractions. The core philosophy is that everything is data, and that data is syntax. Semantics only emerge through interpretation."]
[031m-                   [0m
[032m+[0m
                    [:p "In " [:code "yin.vm"] ", the virtual machine for executing portable code, IO is handled using this exact IO Monad pattern, but without the opacity. When a continuation in " [:code "yin.vm"] " needs to interact with the outside world (like writing to a stream or mutating a cell), it does not actually perform the side-effect. As implemented in the VM's source code, pure functions simply return an effect constructor (via " [:code "make-effect"] "). Rather than yielding an opaque runtime type, the continuation emits a literal, inspectable plain-data effect descriptor. The VM engine then acts as the outer interpreter that reads and executes these returned effects."]
[031m-                   [0m
[032m+[0m
                    [:p "Because a host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call, the agent code completely detaches from the execution. It simply appends its intent to the stream and pauses."]
[031m-                   [0m
[032m+[0m
                    [:h2 "Stigmergic Coordination"]
[031m-                   [0m
[032m+[0m
                    [:p "Forcing side effects to exist as transparent, plain data on a stream unlocks stigmergic coordination. A stream has no privileged reader. Because the intent is just syntax, multiple independent interpreters can observe it simultaneously:"]
[031m-                   [0m
[032m+[0m
                    [:ul
                     [:li "The host adapter reads the descriptor and performs the actual physical network request."]
                     [:li "An audit interpreter can read the exact same descriptor and record it for compliance."]
[031m-                    [:li "A test interpreter intercepts the descriptor and injects a mock response back onto the stream, never touching the physical network."][0m
[031m-                   ][0m
[031m-                   [0m
[032m+                    [:li "A test interpreter intercepts the descriptor and injects a mock response back onto the stream, never touching the physical network."]][0m
[032m+[0m
                    [:p "If a traditional opaque IO monad were used, the auditor and the test suite would have to be coupled to the execution layer. Turning the IO monad into transparent data on dao.stream lets the system emerge from independent interpreters of pure data."]
[031m-                   [0m
[032m+[0m
                    [:h2 "Related Articles"]
[031m-                   [0m
[032m+[0m
